-- lib/repeats-kasai.cub — Kasai's LCP (Kasai, Lee, Arimura, Arikawa, Park 2001) over a rank list, for PR #10
-- item 3 (index-every-repeated-sequence). The suffixes are walked in text order; each one's predecessor in the
-- suffix order is found in a search tree keyed by rank (lib/repeats-fast.cub's RTree), and its common prefix
-- with that predecessor is computed from the last one's less one (Kasai's invariant), so no prefix is compared
-- twice past the carried bound. What is here, each proved for every text and every rank list that orders the
-- suffixes (FinalP: ranks-ok for prefix doubling, rank-prop for the merge-sorted key tree):
--   kasai-step    Kasai's invariant: if p is the predecessor of s = x · s' with common prefix h, the
--                 predecessor of s' shares at least h - 1 with s' (k-kasai, from k-btw: between a and c in
--                 the order, b shares with c at least what a shares).
--   k-skip        the common prefix computed from a known lower bound is the common prefix.
--   k-tree        the tree's predecessor query is the last smaller element of the sorted list.
--   kasai-by-ok   every entry (r, p, s, l) of the output: l is lcp p s, s is a suffix, p is a suffix
--                 below s and every suffix below s is at most p (p is s's neighbour in the suffix array);
--                 so each l is the common prefix that can go no further (lcp-common, lcp-stops).
--   kasai-ok      for the prefix-doubling ranks; kasai-ms-ok for the merge-sorted key tree's ranks.
import "repeats-fast.hypercubical"

